<osm version="0.6" generator="hand-written">
  <!--
    A hand-made map of a few made-up streets near Mayfield, Newcastle, for the router-road tests.
    Not real roads. (GraphHopper wants <osm> before anything else, so this note is inside it.)

    Grid: 4 x 4 corners, rows about 100 m apart (0.0009 degrees of latitude), columns about 100 m
    apart (0.00107 degrees of longitude). Node id = 1 + row * 4 + column, row 0 in the north,
    column 0 in the west.
      row 0  -32.8970     column 0  151.73600
      row 1  -32.8979     column 1  151.73707
      row 2  -32.8988     column 2  151.73814
      row 3  -32.8997     column 3  151.73921
    - Rows 0, 1 and 3 and every column are residential streets.
    - Row 1 is one-way, west to east (5 to 8).
    - Row 2 is a primary road, running one column further out on each side (nodes 17 and 18).
    - A footway runs diagonally from corner 13 (south-west) to corner 4 (north-east), through
      node 19, joined to no street in between.
    - An island: a drivable square about 1.8 km south (nodes 31 to 34), linked to nothing.
  -->
  <node id="1" version="1" lat="-32.8970" lon="151.73600"/>
  <node id="2" version="1" lat="-32.8970" lon="151.73707"/>
